{
    "TRAVEL_DAWN": [
        "I rose before the light and set out. The road was mine alone.",
        "The sky turned pale as I walked. Another step, another mile.",
        "Dawn broke cold on the path. I kept moving.",
        "First light found me on the road. I did not wait for the sun.",
        "The world woke around me, but I was already walking.",
        "Grey light, wet grass, a road that asked nothing. I gave it my feet.",
        "The birds started before I did, but not by much.",
        "Dawn is the road's honest hour. Nothing has had time to lie yet.",
        "I left while the smoke of last night's fire still hung in the air.",
        "Cold hands, warm blood, first light. Walking.",
    ],
    "TRAVEL_MORNING": [
        "The morning was bright and the road stretched ahead.",
        "I walked through the early hours. The light was kind.",
        "The day opened before me. I walked into it.",
        "The dew burned off and the miles came easy.",
        "A good morning for walking. There are not so many; I notice them.",
        "The road was busy with small lives this morning — beetles, larks, one fox.",
        "I ate as I walked. The morning did not mind.",
        "Mist in the hollows, sun on the rises. I walked through both.",
        "The morning was honest work — one foot, then the other, the dew burning off the grass.",
        "Birdsong and a cool road. I made good distance before the heat could find an opinion.",
        "The day was young and so, briefly, was I. The miles went easy.",
        "Mist still in the hollows, the sun just clearing the trees. I walked into the warm of it.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_NOON": [
        "The sun beat down. I walked the midpoint of the day.",
        "No rest at noon. The road does not pause.",
        "High sun, long shadow beneath me. I kept walking.",
        "I measured the day by my shadow and found it short.",
        "Noon is when the road is longest. I walked it anyway.",
        "I rested in the shade of a stone for a hundred breaths, then went on.",
        "The heat sat on my shoulders like a pack. I carried both.",
        "The sun stood straight overhead and judged me. I kept walking out of spite.",
        "Midday, and the road shimmered. I drank, rested a breath, and went on.",
        "High noon and no shade to bargain with. I made my hat earn its keep.",
        "The day at its full height, everything wearing the short honest shadow of an open ledger.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_AFTERNOON": [
        "The afternoon wore on. I walked the long hours.",
        "Shadows lengthened ahead of me. I followed them.",
        "The day turned. I did not turn with it.",
        "The light went gold and the dust with it.",
        "Long hours. The kind that pass through me rather than by me.",
        "I counted milestones until I stopped counting. Then I just walked.",
        "The afternoon asked nothing and gave little. Fair trade.",
        "The light went long and amber. The day was spending itself and I was its coin.",
        "Afternoon settled over the road like dust. I let it settle on me too and walked through.",
        "The heat came off the day's high and the road eased. I eased with it.",
        "Shadows began to lean east. I leaned with them, toward whatever the evening held.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_DUSK": [
        "The light was failing when I finally stopped walking.",
        "Dusk caught me between places. I pressed on.",
        "The road grew dark. I did not stop.",
        "Shadows lengthened across my path. I followed them.",
        "Evening found me still on the road. I accept that.",
        "The last light caught the tops of the trees and let go.",
        "Day's end. My legs knew it before the sky did.",
        "I walked the seam between day and night and felt at home in it.",
        "The light went red and low and the day folded up behind me. I looked for somewhere to stop.",
        "Dusk laid long shadows across the road, mine the longest of them. I kept its company.",
        "The day's last colour bled out of the sky. I quickened my step toward the dark.",
        "Lamps would be lighting in the towns I could not see. I walked toward the idea of them.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_NIGHT": [
        "I walked through the dark. The stars were my only company.",
        "Night on the road is its own kind of quiet. I moved through it.",
        "The world was dark and I was a shadow crossing it.",
        "Only fools travel at night. I kept walking anyway.",
        "The dark road held no comfort. I walked it regardless.",
        "Something kept pace with me in the dark for a while. Then it didn't.",
        "Night walking: half listening, half praying, all forward.",
        "The moon did what it could. I did the rest.",
        "The first stars came out one by one, like a careful man counting his coin.",
        "Night settled over the road and the cold came with it. I pulled my collar and went on.",
        "A half-moon gave me just enough road to trust. I trusted it and little else.",
        "The dark was full of small noises that meant nothing or everything. I chose nothing.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_DEEP_NIGHT": [
        "The world slept. I did not.",
        "Deepest night. I moved through it like a ghost.",
        "The dark was absolute. My steps were the only sound.",
        "Even the wind had gone to bed. I trespassed in the stillness.",
        "The hour when the world forgets itself. I walked through its forgetting.",
        "No light, no sound, no company but my own heartbeat. It was enough.",
        "The cold of the small hours got into my boots. I walked to outrun it and did not.",
        "Stars enough to read by, if the road were a book. It was not. I went by memory.",
        "The night had teeth this far from any hearth. I kept my collar up and my pace honest.",
        "Nothing moved that I could see. I moved anyway — quiet, a rumour crossing the dark.",
        "The kind of dark that makes a man count his own breaths. I lost count and was glad.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_STORM": [
        "The storm found me on the road. I did not stop.",
        "Rain hammered the path. I walked through it.",
        "Wind tried to turn me back. The wind does not decide.",
        "The sky opened up and I kept walking. What else is there to do?",
        "The thunder counted my steps. I lost count of the thunder.",
        "Every tree wanted me under it. I trusted none of them.",
        "Soaked through by the first mile. After that, the rain was just weather.",
        "The storm and I went the same way for a while. Then it lost interest.",
        "The storm threw everything it had and I gave it my back and kept walking.",
        "Rain sideways, the road a river. I waded the worst of it and cursed the rest.",
        "Thunder walked the hills like a landlord. I paid my rent in being soaked through.",
        "The sky opened and did not apologise. I found no shelter and made do with stubbornness.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_SNOW": [
        "Snow covered the road. I made my own path.",
        "The path was white and still. I broke the silence with my steps.",
        "Each step left a mark in the snow. I kept stepping.",
        "The snow asked silence and I gave it. We got along.",
        "White road, grey sky, black trees. The world in three inks.",
        "My breath went ahead of me in little clouds. I followed it.",
        "The snow came down without hurry. I went on without hurry to match it.",
        "Drifts to the knee in the low places. I picked the high line and paid for it in wind.",
        "Every sound was wrapped in wool today. Even my own footsteps were polite.",
        "The flakes settled on my shoulders like the world meant to bury me gently.",
        "White to the horizon, and the horizon not far. I walked the small world it left me.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_FOG": [
        "Fog swallowed the road ahead. I walked into it.",
        "The mist was thick. I trusted my feet.",
        "I could barely see. I walked anyway.",
        "Shapes came and went in the mist. I greeted none of them.",
        "The fog made the familiar strange. I made my legs ignore it.",
        "Ten paces of road at a time. The world rationed itself today.",
        "The road gave me ten feet and asked for faith for the rest. I gave it.",
        "Grey on grey on grey. I navigated by the feel of the ruts underfoot.",
        "Off in the murk a bird complained. I agreed with it and kept walking.",
        "The fog had no edges and neither did the morning. I made my own.",
        "I lost the horizon, then the field, then nearly the road. I kept the road.",
    
    ],
    "TRAVEL_CLEAR_DAWN": [
        "A clear dawn. The road stretched out before me like a promise.",
        "The morning was clean and bright. I took it as a sign.",
        "Sky like washed glass. Days like this are owed to someone.",
        "The horizon was sharp enough to cut. I walked toward the wound.",
        "Not a cloud. The land lay open like a ledger.",
        "First light, no wind — the kind of morning that makes promises it will not keep.",
        "The sun came up clean over the treeline. I let it warm the side of my face and walked.",
        "A dawn without a cloud to its name. I distrusted it and went anyway.",
        "Light came slantwise and gold, and the frost gave it back, every blade lit.",
        "The day opened like a held breath let go. I matched my pace to it.",
        "Cold, clear, and quiet. The road ahead was a long sentence with no comma in it.",
    
    ],
    "ENCOUNTER_TEMPLATES": [
        "Another face on the road. Words were exchanged. That is all.",
        "Someone crossed my path. We spoke. The world is full of such moments.",
        "I met a stranger. The meeting was brief. It stays with me.",
        "An encounter. Brief, but not without weight.",
        "The road provides company, sometimes. Today it did — for a moment.",
        "The road put someone in my way. We managed it, both of us.",
        "Faces on the road: each one a country I will never visit twice.",
        "We traded a few words and kept our distances. Civilization, of a kind.",
        "They went their way, I went mine. The road held us both a moment.",
        "Some meetings weigh nothing. This one had a little weight to it.",
        "I keep a count of kindnesses met on the road. It moved today — one way or the other.",
    ],
    "REST_CAMPFIRE": [
        "The fire is small. I am tired. I sleep.",
        "I built a fire and watched it until my eyes closed.",
        "The flames are low. The night is long. I rest.",
        "A small fire. A small comfort. I sleep.",
        "The fire crackles. I let myself close my eyes.",
        "Fed the fire twigs until it trusted me. Slept in its good opinion.",
        "The fire talked all night, soft. I fell asleep mid-sentence.",
        "One ember watched me till morning. I let it.",
        "A small fire, a smaller meal, the dark held off at arm's length. It was enough.",
        "I fed the fire twigs and the fire fed me a little courage. We kept each other.",
        "The flames talked to themselves and I let them. Sleep came warm-sided and brief.",
        "Embers and the smell of woodsmoke in my coat. I slept the careful sleep of the open road.",
    
    ],
    "REST_OUTSIDE": [
        "No fire tonight. I sleep with one eye open.",
        "The ground is hard. The sky is wide. I lie down anyway.",
        "I sleep in the open. The cold is an old companion.",
        "No shelter. I rest against the earth and hope for dawn.",
        "The stars are my roof. It is not enough. I sleep anyway.",
        "I slept like the stones around me: badly, and watching.",
        "Every sound was a wolf until it wasn't. Then I slept.",
        "The earth is an honest bed. It promises nothing and keeps its word.",
        "Curled up small, the way the old road-wisdom says. Still cold.",
        "No roof but the sky's, and the sky in a generous mood. I slept light and woke whole.",
        "The cold ground took its tax from my bones. I paid it and rose stiff and grateful.",
        "I lay back and let the stars do the watching. They are reliable, if distant, company.",
        "A hollow out of the wind, a flat stone for a pillow. The body forgives a great deal.",
    
    ],
    "REST_SETTLEMENT": [
        "A roof overhead. The floor is hard but it is warm.",
        "I sleep under a proper roof. The walls hold back the world.",
        "The settlement takes me in. I rest on solid ground.",
        "Shelter. The simple kind. I am grateful.",
        "Four walls and a bed. It is enough.",
        "Other people's sounds through the wall: someone's cough, someone's child. I slept among lives.",
        "The floor smelled of woodsmoke and dogs. Better than most nights.",
        "They let me sleep by the wall. The wall and I are grateful.",
        "Four walls not my own, but walls. I slept the easy sleep of the briefly safe.",
        "A borrowed corner and the murmur of strangers' lives. I rested in the noise of other people.",
        "The town breathed around me all night — a cough, a closing door, a dog's brief argument.",
        "Under someone else's roof, in someone else's town. I took the kindness and slept.",
    
    ],
    "REST_INN": [
        "A proper bed. Soft linen. I sleep the sleep of the fortunate.",
        "The inn is warm. The mattress yields. I let myself rest deeply.",
        "For tonight, I am not on the road. I sleep without fear.",
        "Heat from the hearth. A blanket. I close my eyes and do not dream.",
        "The innkeeper asked no questions. I gave no answers. I slept well.",
        "I paid for the bed and the lock on the door. The lock slept better than either of us.",
        "Clean straw, near-clean blanket. Luxury keeps low standards on the road, thankfully.",
        "Somebody sang in the common room until late. I fell asleep inside the song.",
        "A real bed, or near enough. I lay in it like a coin in a deep pocket and did not stir.",
        "Ale, a fire, a mattress with most of its straw. The road forgave me for one whole night.",
        "The inn was loud till late and quiet after. I found the quiet and kept it.",
        "Clean linen and a bolted door. I slept like a man with nothing left to lose for one night.",
    
    ],
    "REST_LEAN_TO": [
        "A lean-to shields me from the worst of it. I sleep lightly.",
        "The wind is quieter here. I close my eyes against the rough wood.",
        "Rough shelter, but shelter. I rest.",
        "The lean-to creaks. I sleep anyway.",
        "My own work between me and the sky. It held.",
        "The lean-to took the rain's first argument and most of its second.",
        "Knees to chest under slanted wood. Sleep came sideways but it came.",
        "Slanted boards and a thin fire — more than the open sky offered, and I took it.",
        "I propped my pack for a pillow and the lean-to for a roof. Both held till morning.",
        "The fire burned down to an argument of embers. I slept before it finished.",
        "Wind found the gaps and told me about each one. I thanked it and dozed regardless.",
    
    ],
    "DREAM_TEMPLATES": [
        "I dreamed of a vast archive. The shelves went on forever.",
        "In my sleep, a voice spoke from the deep. I did not understand it.",
        "I dreamed of walking a road that had no end. I was not tired.",
        "The dream was of water — cold, clear, rising. I did not drown.",
        "A figure stood at the crossroads in my dream. It pointed. I did not follow.",
        "I dreamed the mountain was hollow and full of slow bells.",
        "In the dream I owed someone a name. I woke before I paid.",
        "I dreamed of the sea swallowing a library, shelf by shelf, patient as tide.",
        "A dream of hands building a wall, my hands, a wall I have never seen. It needed one more stone.",
        "I dreamed I was the road. Everyone I have ever met walked over me, gently.",
        "I dreamed of a city under the lake, its windows still lit, and someone in each one waiting for me to remember their name.",
        "In sleep I held a stone that was warm, and the warmth was a voice, and the voice was older than the stone.",
        "I dreamed the Five sat at a long table and one chair was mine, and I could not tell if I was guest or debt.",
        "A door in a hillside opened in my dream and the dark inside breathed out, slow and patient, like something that had waited a long while.",
        "I dreamed of a snowfall that did not land — each flake hanging — and the silence of it followed me up out of sleep.",
        "In the dream a river ran uphill, carrying lanterns, and I knew without asking I was meant to follow it home.",
    
    ],
    "SCAR_TEMPLATES": [
        "I will not forget the teeth.",
        "The wound is closed. The memory is open.",
        "Pain teaches. This lesson is carved in skin.",
        "Another mark. Another story I do not tell.",
        "The body heals. The body remembers.",
        "Some debts are written on the skin so the heart can stop holding them.",
        "It aches before storms now. I own a small oracle.",
        "I tell people it was a beast. Some days that is even true.",
        "The mark has faded to the color of memory. Both still pull.",
        "The cold got into the old break and stayed. The body keeps the calendar the mind would rather lose.",
        "It healed crooked, and every change in the weather reads me the bill.",
        "There is a white line where the world tried to open me. It did not get far, and I remember exactly how far.",
        "A scar earned, not merely given — and it taught me to be quicker than the thing that made it.",
        "The skin closed over it; the wariness did not. I walk a little turned-away from that kind of trouble now.",
    
    ],
    // Rumors that name the wider world — places and affairs from the canon
    // gazetteer and the post-Fall chronicle, so tavern talk reaches past the horizon.
    "CANON_RUMORS": [
        "They say the ring-market of Velkarath still stands half empty, and the Remnant still calls it a capital.",
        "A carter swears the Karsath Bridge holds yet — seven thousand years, and the western span never cracked.",
        "Word from the steppe: the Wells-Compact closed another well to outsiders. The She'ar count, and remember.",
        "A pilgrim claims the old waystones still line the Pilgrimage Road, mile on mile, pointing at nothing.",
        "Talk of the Tähti coast: five free cities left of the twenty, and each one swears it will outlast the rest.",
        "They say the Archive at Sampsara opened another sealed wing. They said that last year too.",
        "A trader out of the mountains speaks of the great elevator at Oltzafell — still running, water-weighted, older than any kingdom.",
        "Someone's cousin saw the forest edge at Kaelva. Burned ground grows back strange, they say.",
        "The Tidal Isles confederation raised harbor dues again. The Inland Sea grumbles and pays.",
        "Old folk still name the Grey Cough when a winter runs wet. Three bad years, and the quarantine sheds stand empty since.",
        "A peddler swears the Deep-galleries trade fair dealt in copper this season — and that the Tzäkhar counted faster than he could.",
        "Down from Fort Verath: the treaty holds, the garrison drills, and both flags fly over one gate.",
        "They tell of a waystation on the western route where the message-register simply stops mid-line.",
        "A bargeman out of Sampa Crossing says the granaries stand full to the rafters — fifty thousand mouths and grain to spare.",
        "Word from Vessenath: the Masa temples cleared more contracts this season than any year since the Fall.",
        "Halkess set the grain price again. The whole Inland Sea pays it, and grumbles, and pays.",
        "A salvager out of Velkarath sells imperial doorstones by the cartload. The old capital is its own quarry now.",
        "They say Keuramark's rope-bridges carried fifteen thousand through the last frost without one fall.",
        "A drover claims the road to Sampa Crossing is wagons nose to tail for the last ten miles. Nobody doubts him.",
        "A bargeman says the River Sampa leagues settled their toll war without a single drawn knife. Nobody quite believes it.",
        "Steppe traders speak of the aurora as a voice. They do not say whose.",
    ],
    "UNCANNY_RUMORS": [
        "A hunter out of the high woods chased an elk three days and came back not knowing how. Old light through the trunks, he says now, and will say no more.",
        "A carter swears there's a fire kept at the cave-mouth on the pass, and an old woman who wants paying for the road past. He went the long way, and was glad to.",
        "Word from the high country: the scree moved on its own, slow and far too large, and went still the moment the shepherd looked. The mountain settling, surely.",
        "A fisherman won't go past the far reach anymore — says a back broke the water, big as a barn, and the birds went quiet over it. A reef, the others tell him.",
        "They say a grey shape keeps to the deep wood, always upwind, and those who cross it take a fever by dusk. The wet and the cold, surely.",
        "A girl saw someone standing in the shallows at dusk, pale and patient, just past the reeds. A heron, her mother said. The girl won't go to the water now.",
        "They found a single dark bird on the still water the morning old Tuomas died. It didn't startle when they came. A swan strayed far from its fellows, surely.",
        "A stag stood wrong at the treeline, a drover says — antlers too wide, eyes too still — and did not run. He turned his cart around. You would have too.",
    ],
    "RUMOR_TEMPLATES": [
        "I heard something. Whether it is true, I cannot say.",
        "A whisper reached me. I carry it now.",
        "They spoke of something. I listened. That is all.",
        "Rumor travels faster than people. It found me.",
        "Words from a stranger's mouth. I file them away.",
        "Talk is the one crop that grows in every season.",
        "I bought a drink and was paid in hearsay. Fair rate.",
        "Half of what I heard tonight is false. The trick is which half.",
        "News wears out its boots fast on these roads. This piece still had sole left.",
    ],
    // Omens: signs the world shows, leaning with a life's hidden fortune but
    // never proving it. The omen reads fate; it does not change it.
    "OMENS_FAIR": [
        "At dawn the fire burned blue and clean, with no breath behind it. The old folk call that fortune walking close.",
        "A crow crossed my path left to right and did not call. The sensible say it means nothing. The grandmothers say it means the road is kind.",
        "I found my bootlaces tied the way I never tie them, and the morning went easy after. I have decided to take the hint.",
        "The first bird I heard sang three times and stopped. Three is the lucky count, if you believe the counting.",
        "A white hair lay across my pack, not mine and not anyone's. A blessing-thread, my mother would have said, and smiled.",
        "The well-water came up sweet and the bucket light. Small things, but they were on my side today.",
        "A ring of pale mushrooms stood unbroken by the path. Step around it and the day keeps its promise, they say.",
        "The wind turned to my back the moment I set out, as if the land wanted me gone the easy way.",
        "A white moth came to my fire and did not burn — rode my pack a mile, then was gone. The grandmothers would call that a blessing minding its manners.",
        "Three magpies on the waymark, and the road ran easy after. I will not pretend I did not count them.",
        "The wind turned to my back at the worst hill and pushed. The sensible call it weather; I called it kindness and climbed.",
        "I woke before dawn for no reason, in time to see the sky go rose and gold. Some mornings the world shows you its good side on purpose.",
        "A coin I thought long lost turned up in a seam of my coat. Small luck — but luck keeps its own books, and today the ledger ran my way.",
        "Still water at the ford, and my reflection looked back unworried. The old folk say a calm face on the water means a calm week.",
    
    ],
    "OMENS_ILL": [
        "The fire would not catch clean — it guttered green and sullen, and the smoke followed me whichever way I turned. An ill sign, if you hold with signs.",
        "A crow crossed right to left and called twice. Nothing, says the head. Turn back, says the older part of me.",
        "I spilled the salt at dawn and could not gather all of it. The grandmothers would have made me throw a pinch and start the day again.",
        "A single magpie sat on the dead branch and watched me pass. One for sorrow, the rhyme goes, and it did not find a second.",
        "My shadow fell wrong in the morning light — too long, too thin, reaching the way I did not want to go.",
        "The dogs in the last village would not look at me, and one backed away growling low. They know things the eyes miss.",
        "I broke a lace, then a strap, then dropped the flint twice. Three small breakages before noon. The count is bad and I know it.",
        "A cold thread of wind came from no quarter at all and raised the hair on my arms. The land was warning someone. I hoped it was not me.",
        "A dog watched me from a doorway and would not be called off, and would not bark. I left that town a little faster than I came.",
        "The milk had turned though the morning was cold, and the bread came out heavy as a stone. Small wrongnesses — but they gather.",
        "I dreamed my own name called from a field, and woke to no field and no caller. The grandmothers say do not answer such a voice. I had not.",
        "A single magpie on the gate, and it would not fly. One for sorrow, the rhyme goes. I touched iron and went on.",
        "The candle drowned in its own wax with hours of wick to spare — an ending before its time, the old folk would say. I lit another and did not love the dark.",
        "Crows on every fence-post the length of the lane, silent, turned to watch me pass. The head says birds. The blood says hurry.",
    
    ],
}
